Name the EARLY_BOOT functions by number

The startup phase finishes and the kernel does not reach bsd_init.
The last startup landmark on the screen names the phase and the
entry, and the entry's number says which function it is -- but the
number is four digits and it is read off a photograph of a phone
held by hand, so it is the hardest thing on the screen to read.

Each of the EARLY_BOOT functions prints a two digit number of its
own instead, so the last one on the screen is two digits and nothing
else:

    01  source_pthread_bootstrap
    02  telemetry_init
    03  set_awl_scratch_exists_flag_and_subscribe_for_pm
    04  iotrace_init
    05  pgz_init
    06  kmem_crypto_init
    07  vm_pageout_create_gc_thread
    08  vm_deferred_reclamation_init
    09  personas_bootstrap
    10  kcov_init

One format string for all of them: the compiler merges the identical
literals, so a marker costs the instructions and nothing else.  The
console blob lives in the tail padding of __TEXT_EXEC and the space
there is what every one of these markers has to fit into.

---
